{"name":"Canvas Cloth","hex":"#e6dfd2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"canvas","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of coarse cloth, woven from hemp (traditionally) or from cotton and polyesters, useful for making sails, tents, and overcoats or as a surface for paintings.","A piece of such cloth stretched across a frame on which one may paint an artwork."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover (an area or object) with canvas."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/canvas"},{"term":"cloth","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fabric, usually made of woven, knitted, or felted fibres or filaments, such as used in dressing, decorating, cleaning or other practical use. Sometimes, woven fabric specifically.","Specifically, a tablecloth, especially as spread before a meal or removed afterwards."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cloth"}],"entries":[{"title":"Canvas","blocks":[{"text":"Canvas is a durable plain-woven fabric used for making sails, tents, marquees, backpacks, shelters, as a support for oil painting and for other items for which sturdiness is required, as well as in such fashion objects as handbags, electronic device cases, and shoes. It is popularly used by artists as a painting surface, typically stretched across a wooden frame."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Sj%C3%B6manss%C3%A4ck_-_1991.jpg/330px-Sj%C3%B6manss%C3%A4ck_-_1991.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canvas","wikidata":"Q4259259"},{"title":"Textile","blocks":[{"text":"A textile is any of various fibre-based materials, including fibres, yarns, filaments, threads, and different types of fabric. Originally, textiles were woven; however, weaving is not the only manufacturing method, and many other methods were later developed to form textile structures based on their intended use. Knitting and non-woven are other popular types of fabric manufacturing. In the contemporary world, textiles satisfy the material needs for versatile applications, from simple daily clothing to bulletproof jackets, spacesuits, doctor's gowns and technical applications like geotextiles."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Warp_in_Progress.jpg/330px-Warp_in_Progress.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Textile","wikidata":"Q28823"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/MetroBrussel_Erasmus.jpg/500px-MetroBrussel_Erasmus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/MetroBrussel_Erasmus.jpg/500px-MetroBrussel_Erasmus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Canvas roof at the Erasmus station of the Brussels Metro","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMetroBrussel_Erasmus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Bitwa_pod_Grunwaldem_Muzeum_Narodowe_05.JPG/500px-Bitwa_pod_Grunwaldem_Muzeum_Narodowe_05.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Bitwa_pod_Grunwaldem_Muzeum_Narodowe_05.JPG/1280px-Bitwa_pod_Grunwaldem_Muzeum_Narodowe_05.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Bitwa_pod_Grunwaldem_Muzeum_Narodowe_05.JPG/500px-Bitwa_pod_Grunwaldem_Muzeum_Narodowe_05.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"One of Poland's biggest canvas paintings, the Battle of Grunwald, 1878, by Jan Matejko (426 cm × 987 cm (168 in × 389 in)), displayed in the National Museum in Warsaw[1]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABitwa_pod_Grunwaldem_Muzeum_Narodowe_05